WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.411
Scoring forecasters: the Brier score.

2
00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:06.168
Timeline claims are forecasts, and forecasts can be scored.

3
00:00:06.448 --> 00:00:18.715
The standard tool is the Brier score: for each forecast, take the stated probability p and the outcome o (1 if it happened, 0 if not), and average the squared error (p minus o) squared.

4
00:00:18.995 --> 00:00:20.147
Lower is better.

5
00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:23.691
A forecaster who always says 50% scores 0.

6
00:00:23.971 --> 00:00:27.299
25 on any set of yes-or-no events, so 0.

7
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:35.579
25 is the no-skill baseline: a forecaster who scores worse than that would have done better by saying “I don’t know” every time.

8
00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:37.075
Two lessons follow.

9
00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:43.435
First, confidence is costly when wrong: a 90% forecast that fails contributes 0.

10
00:00:43.715 --> 00:00:44.611
81.

11
00:00:44.891 --> 00:00:50.672
Bold forecasters who are often wrong score badly even if they are spectacularly right sometimes.

12
00:00:50.952 --> 00:00:56.179
Second, you can only score forecasters who state probabilities on many checkable events.

13
00:00:56.459 --> 00:01:05.248
Most public AI claims are single, unhedged or vaguely hedged statements, which is why the previous lessons rewrote them before scoring.

14
00:01:05.528 --> 00:01:11.779
If someone wants to be taken seriously as a forecaster, the best evidence is a public, scored track record.
